<h2>Step 4: Set Your Budget and Bidding Strategy</h2>

<p>For beginners in Kenya, start with a daily budget of KES 500–1,500 (roughly $3.50–$10 USD). This is enough to gather data without risking large spend.</p>

<h3>Bidding Strategies</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Maximise Clicks</strong>: Google automatically sets bids to get as many clicks as possible within your budget. Good for beginners building traffic.</li>
  <li><strong>Target CPA (Cost Per Acquisition)</strong>: You tell Google what you want to pay per conversion and it optimises accordingly. Use this once you have at least 30 conversions tracked.</li>
  <li><strong>Manual CPC</strong>: You set your own bids per keyword. Most control but requires experience. Not recommended for beginners.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 5: Set Up Conversion Tracking — Non-Negotiable</h2>

<p>Conversion tracking tells Google what a "success" looks like for your campaign — a form submission, a phone call, a WhatsApp click, a purchase. Without it, you are flying blind.</p>

<ol>
  <li>In Google Ads, go to Tools > Conversions > New Conversion Action</li>
  <li>Choose "Website" and install the Google tag on your site</li>
  <li>Set up a conversion action for your "Thank You" page or form submission</li>
  <li>Test it — submit the form yourself and confirm the conversion fires</li>
</ol>

<h2>Step 6: Add Negative Keywords</h2>

<p>Negative keywords prevent your ad from showing for irrelevant searches. This is one of the highest-impact optimisations you can make. Common negatives to add immediately:</p>

<ul>
  <li>"free" — unless you offer something free, exclude this</li>
  <li>"jobs", "careers", "vacancy" — you sell services, not jobs</li>
  <li>"DIY", "how to" — these are research queries, not buying intent</li>
  <li>Competitor names — unless you specifically want to target their searches</li>
</ul>

<h2>Realistic Kenya Google Ads Benchmarks</h2>

<p>Based on campaigns we run in Kenya across various industries:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Average CPC</strong>: KES 25–150 depending on industry (legal and finance are highest)</li>
  <li><strong>Average CTR</strong>: 3–8% for well-optimised ads</li>
  <li><strong>Average conversion rate</strong>: 2–8% for landing pages with clear CTAs</li>
  <li><strong>Cost per lead</strong>: KES 300–2,000 for most service businesses</li>
</ul>

<blockquote>Your first 30 days of Google Ads is a data-gathering exercise, not a profit-making exercise. Expect to spend KES 15,000–30,000 learning what works before you start optimising for ROI. Budget for this learning phase.</blockquote>

